From: anthonybartczak <49080651+anthonybartczak@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 20:11:30 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] Keep the events stream on message boundaries

Now and then an HX Effects (firmware 3.80) sends one notification with
its first four bytes behind the rest of it. The stream reader then
takes a body for a length: `tonepush watch` ends with "stream message
declares 1779853698 bytes", or the reader waits for a 262144-byte
message while every later notification queues behind it.

- `realign` sits between the events channel and its stream reader. It
  passes on whole messages that start `00 00 04 00` (or service 0, as
  one captured service-open reply does), declare at most 4 KB and have
  a body that decodes, as an RPC message on service 4. Anything else
  is stepped over a byte at a time. A slip costs the one notification
  it cut through.
- The body check is needed: stepping through a parameter notification,
  a float 0.0 and the key after it pass as a header.
- `Session::events_skipped` counts the bytes stepped over.
- `fake::Pedal::send_raw_events` sends an events frame as it is, for
  the test that puts such a frame through `poll_notifications`.

Measured on an HX Effects at 3.80 with a Raspberry Pi 4: three minutes
of watching delivered 1135 notifications and stepped over 2961 bytes
without losing the session. All 70 507 messages on the events channel
in the HX Edit captures in this repository pass through.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
